{"title":"2025 USA TST Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_Statement\">Problem Statement</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USA_TST_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem Statement\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be a positive integer.\n</p><p>Prove that one can paint the non-zero coefficients of the polynomial\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/7/20788226bc341cb7f1e7a94ebed2985f8df8b911.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n) = \\prod_{k=0}^n (x_1 + x_2 + \\dots + x_n - k)\\]\" width=\"364\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/4/654a7c7010145200d9d8723eda7ce20e98b5723c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"48\" height=\"13\" /> colors such that the coefficients of each color have a sum 0 and each color is used at least once.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_USA_TST_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The key realization is that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is an arbitrary integer then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/2/8525ed748f2ad862a4d0ffffc1dea2b157f0b82d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0,1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"17\" /> forces <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/c/57ccf8d123ba4259e29fba55d9b008874e071492.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" />. When this happens, the solutions are trivial.\n</p><p>Otherwise, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/3/503728b47daf13610fcee386d9596af1eca39e97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"130\" height=\"20\" /> takes the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/2/32287f4b314da2ce8942f0fcf9c8011d91f956e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x_1+x_2+\\dots+x_n)(\\dots)(x_1+x_2+\\dots+x_n - n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"388\" height=\"20\" />. Indeed, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/0/db0403e028d9eba721d48649ca77bacbf7b8e31a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1 + \\dots + x_n = S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"141\" height=\"17\" /> and this function becomes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/3029331b65a9a92c13a939ebcbe6fcc6d82d88f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(S-1)(S-2)(\\dots)(S-n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"227\" height=\"20\" />. Obviously, it follows that every term in this expansion can choose to include <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/b/67bc6daa9d6b964201d6cef60cbeb1ac5fd26ead.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> or choose not to, hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c36f0b157e8c2fbe0638c2f2b378e08d07695aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> distinct choosing's. If we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/4/654a7c7010145200d9d8723eda7ce20e98b5723c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"48\" height=\"13\" /> colors, by the Pigeonhole Principle, it follows that one color is used twice.\n</p><p>We now prove that a coloring exists such that this common color has the property the coefficients sum itself is 0.\n</p><p>However, the key idea is the polynomial itself! Every term of this polynomial is of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/6/456eb4dc919e192135246e2f049e187ea2e71989.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1^{e_1} x_2^{e_2} \\dots x_n^{e_n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"103\" height=\"19\" />. If one resubstitutes the definition of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, then upon expanding we have that  \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/7/4b704dc1f7dfc4a7f4dbfde278b4b66ef02f6206.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S(S-1)(S-2)(\\dots)(S-n)\\]\" width=\"223\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/3/f535c96b2975c203bc6460b512c9c804a6caf564.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[= S^{n+1} - s_1S^{n} + \\dots +(-1)^n s_n S\\]\" width=\"257\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Where each of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/b/d3b63c17213f84d730e0de1f8c08597f7a648e37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />th symmetric sums of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/1/96177039717541c11c2b769b81c83fd14ad3c00e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,3,\\dots,n \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"115\" height=\"20\" />. \n</p><p>Now, we construct a valid coloring (just like Da Vinci!). Consider an array of colors of subsets from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/5/ad5f0a3130fc3734cf616ffaee9bc935b041e9ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,\\dots,n \\} \\neq \\empty$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"117\" height=\"20\" />. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/6/ae6ccbf37fc2066d95b74b4bfd32cf119c67f740.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{n} - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"16\" /> such colorings. Then, for each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/f/73f841b5d2980f16b8d7586f508fac3232bc2f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_{a_1} x_{a_2} \\dots x_{a_n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"105\" height=\"14\" />, we use the coloring <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc66fe21238a8c3576bc64b45fad3854ccf496ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n \\} \\in \\{1,2,\\dots,n \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"239\" height=\"20\" />. To prove the coefficients of all these terms exist, define:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/f/e6f7ef278c93060a686369d59832bf854cbb46e6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(\\mathbf{1}_A)= \\begin{array}{c} x_i= \\begin{cases} 1,&amp;i\\in A,\\\\ 0,&amp;i\\notin A. \\end{cases} \\end{array}\\]\" width=\"209\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>Where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/1/f41b394bb1707e6bb6bc41238eac51c06eaaa6ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"118\" height=\"20\" />. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/e/fae3aac56f3a092b098bbc84793b30566e8a250f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S=x_1+\\cdots+x_n=|A|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"189\" height=\"20\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/f/bafccdbccd00e47516c731e44637ec96f4dfdb56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x_1,\\dots,x_n)=|A|(|A|-1)\\cdots(|A|-n)=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"353\" height=\"20\" />, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/6/ae64f12294075e261a93212cc28b3aa065d0d129.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\leq |A| \\leq n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" />. If there exists a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/f/d4f8353ff96480f5b94e61b8af9008019c7f4a9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B \\subseteq A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"52\" height=\"15\" />, then let all the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/2/a82ba8404fd6a44addf790ac6e9da9ec9f6c9ac5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i \\in B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"45\" height=\"15\" /> construct <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c75eb8978ee0840709244e93e95693ba1825888.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"17\" /> and otherwise <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/c/eec020b09f33839d9c5190e7ead679afcc68d1c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" />. By definition, this forces a nonzero coefficient <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to form. Otherwise, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/a/76a611b2c768629b6b5ac34d2a874357d849cc1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B = 0 \\implies 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"14\" />. Hence, \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/3/9837918b03150e0b234f05e4754ca6b5fd7fa0cc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(\\mathbf{1}_A)=\\sum_{B\\subseteq A}C_B\\]\" width=\"127\" height=\"40\" />,\n</p><p>However, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/2/332b048c390f06abe4b87d0fa9ab0a6a162e162f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(\\mathbf{1}_A)= 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"81\" height=\"20\" /> by definition, hence the sum is always 0, and thus such a coloring truly does exist, with each color used at least once, such that the sums of the colors equals 0, and the proof is complete. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p><p><b>Remark:</b>\n</p><p>One can question how is it true that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/1/9b10b4cee801dbc0c88d13ff99610229fa680757.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(\\mathbf{1}_A)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"48\" height=\"20\" /> always equals the sum of the coefficients. Actually, recall if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/7/a978db040bb2a542c834649156229a69bc6f7272.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A \\subseteq \\{1,2,\\dots,n \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"135\" height=\"20\" /> then we can make all values <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/7/ed70ca7913c756d1e679c9b280b823f45bc2265c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i \\in A \\implies x_i = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"152\" height=\"17\" />, and otherwise 0. In <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/4/6b4632c53c03dfdddd59b8323a1c6995135a6f25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x_1,x_2,\\dots,x_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"130\" height=\"20\" /> this forces the coefficients of only those terms to be considered. But when we do that, we substitute some number of 1's into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/6/c96dd6ec1dc4ad7520fbdc78fcdbec9edd068d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"37\" height=\"20\" />. But recall <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/9/f49be0d57e73b58cab46696ca454ce847da785bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1 + x_2+ \\dots + x_n = |A|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"193\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/a/31a28cfaf94c8c3dc008d2a7084c836ece3c02c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(|A) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"79\" height=\"20\" /> hence the result is trivial, and that is why the sum of the coefficients is always 0.\n</p>\n<!-